2026-10-27 00:56:16+07:00 [INFO|base_episode_generator.py:176:215] 2026-10-26 17:56:16,409 >> Wait, but why can't it be negative? Because square roots are always non-negative. So the smallest possible value of ||a + b|| is indeed 11 when the angle between a and b is 180 degrees. That would mean they are pointing in opposite directions, which makes their sum's magnitude the difference of their magnitudes. Oh right, because if they are in opposite directions, their vectors subtract, so |a| + |b| is 3 +14=17, but wait, no, wait. Wait, when they are in opposite directions, the magnitude would be |14 -3| =11, which matches our calculation.
